    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- Pull Quote -->
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Blacktop sealer cost is one of the most misunderstood numbers in driveway maintenance. A $28 bucket and a $65 bucket can both be "the right choice" — the difference is coverage rate, chemistry, and how many years you're buying with each dollar. Here's what the sealer product itself actually costs, with the labor and full-project math stripped out.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you've searched "how much does blacktop sealer cost," you've probably found answers that mix in labor, crack repair, and driveway-size assumptions that don't match your project. This guide does one thing: it prices the sealer product itself — dollars per gallon, dollars per 5-gallon bucket, and dollars per square foot of coverage — across the three tiers you'll actually find on a store shelf. If you also want the full project cost with labor, DIY vs. professional pricing, and regional labor rates, our companion guide on <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">how much it costs to seal a driveway</a> covers that ground in depth.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Cost at a Glance -->
        <div id="cost-at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Blacktop Sealer Cost at a Glance
          </h2>
          <p>
            Blacktop sealer is sold almost exclusively in 5-gallon buckets, with per-gallon pricing implied by dividing the bucket price. Products fall into three practical price tiers: economy asphalt emulsion, mid-range polymer-modified emulsion, and premium coal tar or heavy-duty formulas. The table below shows current 2026 retail pricing per gallon and per bucket, along with total materials cost for the three driveway sizes homeowners ask about most.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
            <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Per Gallon</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Per 5-Gal Bucket</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">One-Car (~250 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Two-Car (~500 sq ft)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Large (~1,000 sq ft)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Economy (Asphalt Emulsion)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5–$6</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$25–$30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$25–$30 (1 bucket)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50–$60 (2 buckets)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$75–$90 (3 buckets)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Mid-Range (Polymer-Modified)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$7–$9</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$35–$45</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$35–$45 (1 bucket)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$70–$90 (2 buckets)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$105–$135 (3 buckets)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Premium (Coal Tar / Heavy-Duty)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$11–$14</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$55–$70</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$55–$70 (1 bucket)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$110–$140 (2 buckets)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$220–$280 (4 buckets)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A few patterns are worth calling out immediately. First, the per-gallon price roughly triples from economy to premium — $5–$6 per gallon versus $11–$14 — but the difference isn't padding; it reflects genuinely different chemistry and coverage rate. Second, coverage per bucket declines as you move up in tier: economy sealer covers roughly 400 square feet per 5-gallon bucket, mid-range covers 350–400 square feet, and premium coal tar or heavy-duty formulas cover only 300–350 square feet because the higher solids content lays down a thicker, more protective film per coat. Third, on a large driveway the premium tier requires a fourth bucket where economy and mid-range only need three — this is why the large-driveway premium jump ($220–$280) looks disproportionately larger than the per-gallon price difference alone would suggest.
          </p>
          <p>
            These figures assume a standard two-coat application, which is the industry-recommended method for any blacktop sealer regardless of tier. Buying "enough for one thick coat" is a common and costly mistake — a single heavy coat cures poorly, cracks faster, and typically fails within a year or two, meaning you'll spend the money again sooner than if you'd bought correctly the first time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Price Factors -->
        <div id="price-factors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Determines the Price of Blacktop Sealer
          </h2>
          <p>
            Blacktop sealer pricing isn't arbitrary. Four factors explain nearly all of the variation you'll see between a $26 bucket and a $68 bucket on the same store shelf.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Base Chemistry</h3>
          <p>
            The single biggest cost driver is the raw material itself. Asphalt emulsion — a water-based mixture of asphalt cement, water, and emulsifying agents — is the cheapest to manufacture and the cheapest to buy, which is why it anchors the economy tier. Coal tar emulsion uses coal tar pitch as its binder instead of asphalt, and coal tar is a pricier, less abundant raw material that also requires more careful handling due to fumes and regulatory restrictions. Oil-based (cutback) sealers sit in between, priced closer to mid-range products but formulated for penetration rather than surface film-forming. For a full explanation of how these chemistries differ in application and performance, see our guide to <a href="/blacktop-driveway-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">blacktop driveway sealer</a> products and how to apply them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solids Content</h3>
          <p>
            Solids content — the percentage of the product that remains on the driveway after water evaporates — is the technical spec that separates budget and premium formulas within the same chemistry category. A basic asphalt emulsion sealer might run 35–40% solids. A polymer-modified mid-range product often reaches 45–50% solids. Premium heavy-duty and airport-grade formulas can exceed 50–55% solids. Higher solids content costs more per gallon to manufacture, but it also means more material stays on your driveway as a protective film after each coat cures, which is part of why premium products cover less square footage per bucket despite costing more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Additives and Polymers</h3>
          <p>
            Mid-range and premium sealers frequently include polymer modifiers, fast-dry additives, and UV-stabilizing agents that basic economy formulas skip entirely. These additives add real cost to the manufacturing process but also deliver measurable performance gains — better flexibility through freeze-thaw cycles, faster return-to-traffic times, and slower UV-driven fading. Products marketed with a "rubberized" or "polymer-modified" label on the bucket are almost always priced in the mid-range tier as a direct result of these additives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brand and Retailer Markup</h3>
          <p>
            Finally, brand reputation and retailer channel affect price independent of formulation. A well-known national brand sold at a home improvement chain typically carries a 10–20% premium over a comparable regional or contractor-supply brand with nearly identical solids content and coverage. Buying from a contractor supply yard rather than a big-box retailer can shave several dollars off each bucket, particularly if you're buying multiple buckets for a large driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: What's Included -->
        <div id="whats-included" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What's Actually in "The Sealer Cost" — and What's Not
          </h2>
          <p>
            Every price in this guide so far refers strictly to the sealer product itself — what's inside the bucket. That's a deliberately narrow number, and it's important to understand exactly what it does and doesn't cover before you budget a project around it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-6">What's Inside "The Sealer Cost" vs. What's Extra</p>
            <div class="space-y-4">
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">✓ Included: The sealer product itself</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Asphalt emulsion, polymer-modified, or coal tar formula — priced by the gallon/bucket above</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#38BDF8] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$25–$70/bucket</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">✗ Extra: Crack filler</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Required before sealing on most driveways over a few years old</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$15–$40</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">✗ Extra: Degreaser / oil spot primer</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Non-negotiable wherever oil or gasoline has contaminated the surface</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$10–$30</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">✗ Extra: Applicator tools</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Squeegee, push broom, pump sprayer — mostly reusable across future applications</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$25–$65</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">✗ Extra: Masking supplies</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Painter's tape and plastic sheeting for edges, garage doors, walkways</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">$5–$15</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex justify-between items-start">
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-white">✗ Extra: Application labor (if hiring a pro)</p>
                  <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">Not reflected anywhere in this guide's figures — materials only</p>
                </div>
                <span class="text-[#FF4500] font-black whitespace-nowrap ml-4">Varies</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mt-6 pt-6 border-t border-white/20 flex justify-between items-center">
              <p class="text-xl font-black text-white">Typical Extras Beyond Sealer (Two-Car Driveway)</p>
              <span class="text-2xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">$40–$110</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            In other words, the true out-the-door cost of a DIY blacktop sealing project is the sealer price from the table above, plus roughly $40–$110 in extras depending on your driveway's condition. A two-car driveway using mid-range sealer, for example, runs $70–$90 for the sealer itself and $110–$200 total once crack filler, degreaser, and basic tools are factored in — assuming you don't already own the reusable tools from a previous application. If you want the complete picture — including professional labor rates by region, DIY vs. contractor comparisons, and a full itemized project budget — our guide on <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] hover:underline">how much it costs to seal a driveway</a> covers all of that in detail. This page stays focused specifically on what the sealer product itself costs.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more nuance worth flagging: retailers frequently bundle a small amount of crack filler or sand additive into "all-in-one" sealer buckets and market the combined price as if it were pure sealer cost. Henry HE200, for example, is formulated as a combined sealer-and-filler product specifically so it can address hairline cracking without a separate purchase — read the label carefully, because a bucket priced $8–$10 higher than a comparable pure sealer may already include filler you'd otherwise buy separately anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Cost Per Sq Ft -->
        <div id="cost-per-sqft" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cost Per Square Foot of Materials Only
          </h2>
          <p>
            Cost per square foot is the cleanest way to compare tiers apples-to-apples, because it accounts for both the bucket price and the coverage rate in a single number. Here's how the three tiers break down on a strict materials-only basis, calculated from the coverage and pricing figures above.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Tier</th>
