          </div>

          <p>
            To use this table for your own driveway, measure length times width in feet to get your square footage, then multiply by the appropriate per-square-foot figure. A 24-by-24-foot two-car driveway (576 sq ft) sealed with mid-range polymer-modified sealer costs roughly $52–$69 in materials — round up to account for surface porosity and edge waste, and buy the next full bucket rather than trying to stretch a partial bucket across the whole surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's worth noting how this compares to the broader project-cost figures homeowners often see quoted for driveway sealing overall, which typically include labor and run $0.14–$0.75 per square foot depending on whether it's DIY or professional work. The $0.06–$0.22 range here is materials only — it's the floor of what any driveway sealing project costs, before labor, prep, or extras are added on top.
          </p>
          <p>
            A quick sizing note: driveway square footage varies more than most homeowners expect. A short, narrow single-car driveway can be as small as 150–200 square feet, while a long two-car driveway with a turnaround can exceed 800 square feet. Always measure your actual driveway rather than assuming it matches the "standard" figures used in cost guides — a 20% miscalculation on square footage translates directly into buying the wrong number of buckets.
          </p>
