        <!-- SECTION 5: Cost Per Year -->
        <div id="cost-per-year" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Coal Tar vs. Asphalt Emulsion vs. Oil-Based: Cost Per Year of Protection
          </h2>
          <p>
            Comparing sealers purely on upfront bucket price is misleading, because reseal intervals differ significantly by chemistry. The smarter comparison divides total materials cost by expected years of protection, giving you a true cost-per-year figure. Here's that math applied to a two-car driveway (~500 sq ft) across the three major blacktop sealer chemistries.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-900 text-white">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Sealer Chemistry</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Materials Cost (500 sq ft)</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Typical Reseal Interval</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-sm">Cost Per Year</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Basic Asphalt Emulsion</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50–$60</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2–3 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-green-700">$17–$30/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Polymer-Modified Emulsion</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$70–$90</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3–4 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-green-700">$18–$30/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Oil-Based (Cutback)</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$85–$110</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3–4 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$21–$37/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Coal Tar Emulsion</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$110–$140</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4–5 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$22–$35/yr</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The takeaway is a little counterintuitive: on pure cost-per-year materials math, the four chemistries land in a surprisingly tight band — roughly $17 to $37 per year, with a lot of overlap between tiers. Coal tar's longer reseal interval offsets most, but not all, of its higher upfront price; it doesn't come out dramatically cheaper over time on a materials basis alone. That means the decision between chemistries should be driven primarily by <strong>performance needs</strong> — chemical resistance to oil and gas spills, climate and freeze-thaw exposure, local regulatory restrictions on coal tar — rather than by chasing the lowest theoretical annual cost, because the annual cost differences are modest compared to the performance differences.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where the math changes meaningfully is if a cheaper sealer fails early due to poor surface prep or a skipped step. A basic asphalt emulsion job that peels after 12 months instead of lasting its expected 2–3 years effectively costs $50–$60 for one year of protection — worse than any tier in this table, including premium coal tar. This is the same lesson that applies across every coating category we've analyzed: the sealer's cost per year of protection depends as much on correct application as on which product you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

