          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where to Buy Blacktop Sealer & Price Variations
          </h2>
          <p>
            Where you buy blacktop sealer affects the price you pay almost as much as which tier you choose. Here's how the major channels compare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Home Improvement Chains</h3>
          <p>
            Home Depot, Lowe's, and similar national chains stock the widest selection across all three tiers and are the default choice for most DIYers. Pricing tends to sit at or slightly above the midpoint of the ranges in this guide, reflecting the convenience and consistent availability. These stores also run seasonal sales — typically in early spring before sealing season ramps up, and occasionally in early fall as they clear inventory — where you can find 10–20% discounts on multi-bucket purchases.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Contractor Supply Yards</h3>
          <p>
            Paving and contractor supply yards sell the same or very similar products, often at a modest discount from big-box pricing, especially if you're buying three or more buckets. Some yards will only sell in case quantities, so this channel makes the most sense for large driveways or if you're splitting a bulk purchase with a neighbor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Online Retailers</h3>
          <p>
            Buying blacktop sealer online is less common than in-person purchase because of the weight and shipping cost of a 5-gallon bucket, but it can work out cheaper for premium and specialty products that aren't stocked locally. Factor in shipping cost when comparing an online price to a local store price — a sealer that looks $8 cheaper online can end up costing more once freight is added for a heavy liquid product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying in Bulk for Multiple Buckets</h3>
          <p>
            If your driveway requires three or more buckets, ask about case pricing at both big-box stores and contractor yards — many retailers offer a per-bucket discount once you cross that threshold, since it reduces their per-transaction handling cost. On a large driveway needing three or four buckets of premium sealer, a 10% bulk discount can save $20–$30, which is worth a five-minute question at checkout.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-700 mb-2">Buying Tip</p>
            <p class="text-amber-900">Always buy slightly more sealer than your calculated need — an extra half-bucket, not a whole extra one. Surface porosity varies across even a single driveway, and older, more oxidized asphalt absorbs noticeably more sealer than newer pavement. Running out mid-application forces an emergency store run and often leaves a visible seam where application resumed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does blacktop sealer cost per gallon or bucket?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Blacktop sealer costs $5–$6 per gallon ($25–$30 per 5-gallon bucket) for economy asphalt emulsion, $7–$9 per gallon ($35–$45 per bucket) for mid-range polymer-modified emulsion, and $11–$14 per gallon ($55–$70 per bucket) for premium coal tar or heavy-duty formulas. Price per gallon isn't the whole story — coverage rate varies by tier, so a cheaper bucket that covers less area can end up costing more per square foot than a pricier one that covers more.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does blacktop sealer cost to seal an entire driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For a one-car driveway (about 250 sq ft), expect to spend $25–$70 on sealer alone depending on tier. A standard two-car driveway (about 500 sq ft) runs $50–$140 in materials. A large driveway (around 1,000 sq ft) costs $75–$280. These figures cover the sealer only — budget separately for crack filler, degreaser, and applicator tools, which typically add another $40–$100.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is more expensive blacktop sealer worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Usually yes, but the margin is thinner than the sticker price suggests. On a cost-per-year-of-protection basis, economy asphalt emulsion runs about $17–$30 per year, mid-range polymer-modified emulsion runs $18–$30 per year, and premium coal tar runs $22–$35 per year — coal tar's longer service life mostly offsets its higher upfront price rather than delivering dramatically cheaper long-term ownership. Premium sealer earns its cost primarily through better chemical resistance to gas and oil spills and a thicker, more durable film, not through lower annual cost.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do coal tar sealers cost more than asphalt emulsion sealers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Coal tar sealers cost more because the raw material itself is pricier than asphalt emulsion, the manufacturing process requires more careful handling due to fumes and PAH content, and coal tar delivers superior chemical resistance that commands a premium in the marketplace. You're also paying for a longer reseal interval — 4–5 years versus 2–3 years for standard emulsion — which is baked into the higher per-bucket price. Coal tar sealers are also banned in several states and municipalities, including Washington and Minnesota, so limited availability affects price in some regions too.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the cost of blacktop sealer include labor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">No. Every price in this guide is for materials only — the sealer product itself, not application labor. If you hire a professional, labor typically adds $0.10–$0.50 per square foot on top of materials, pushing a two-car driveway project to $125–$450 installed. DIY application avoids that labor cost entirely; a standard two-car driveway takes about 2–4 hours to apply yourself. For the complete cost breakdown including labor, tools, and regional pricing, see our full guide to how much it costs to seal a driveway.</p>
