          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Blacktop Sealing Budget Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing quotes and material estimates both go wrong in predictable ways, usually by leaving out work that turns out to be mandatory.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Budgeting for Sealer and Not for Prep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Crack filler, degreaser, primer for oil spots, and a squeegee or brush add meaningfully to a DIY total, and on a professional quote they are often the line items that separate a cheap bid from a thorough one. Price the whole job, not just the pails.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Coverage Rates Are Achievable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Label coverage assumes smooth, sound pavement. A porous, weathered driveway can absorb 30 to 50 percent more than the number on the pail, and running short partway through a second coat means a visible seam. Buy for your actual surface and expect to have some left over.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Cheapest Quote Without Asking About Coats</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single biggest variable between contractor prices is whether the quote covers one coat or two, and whether cracks are filled first. A one-coat job over unfilled cracks is genuinely cheaper and genuinely worth less. Get the scope in writing before comparing numbers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing More Often Than Necessary</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Annual sealing looks like diligence and costs money while shortening the life of the surface, because layered sealer goes brittle and cracks. Most residential driveways need it every two to four years. Judging by condition rather than by the calendar is the cheapest decision in this whole article.
              </p>
            </div>
